    // The named glyphs of the icon font, as data rather than as C++: the
    // generator emits `enum class FluentSymbol` from this file the way it
    // emits everything else from the metadata and the dictionaries.
    //
    // Why a file at all. There is no machine-readable source for these names
    // anywhere in the SDK -- the font itself carries no glyph names (its
    // `post` table is version 3.0, which has none), and the only list
    // Microsoft publishes is the documentation page. So the names are typed
    // in once, here, where they can be reviewed and corrected, rather than
    // being rediscovered by every reader.
    //
    // Where they come from:
    //
    //   https://learn.microsoft.com/windows/apps/design/iconography/segoe-fluent-icons-font
    //
    // The whole of it -- E700 through F8CC, every glyph the page documents.
    // That page names the font we actually draw with, so it is the base;
    // where the older Segoe MDL2 Assets page names the same code point
    // differently, this one wins. It has to: Fluent moved the EBD4 series to
    // MobWifiCall* and gave the plain WifiCall* names to F657, and taking
    // the older names would put one name on two code points.
    //
    // Two entries do NOT follow that page, because the font disagrees with
    // it. EB0F and EB11 are labelled StockUp and StockDown there, and the
    // glyphs are the other way round -- EB0F is the downward triangle. Drawn
    // and looked at, not reasoned about; the MDL2 page has this pair right.
    //
    // The page itself has small defects of its own, worked around when this
    // was harvested: five rows carry a five-digit code point (e7444 for
    // ResizeMouseMedium, e7999, eb333, eb444, eb777), and six more have had
    // the hex mangled by machine translation. The code point in the row's
    // image file name is intact in every case, and that is what was read.
    //
    // How to check an addition: every code point here must exist in the
    // installed font. Ask DirectWrite rather than the eye. All of these do,
    // in Segoe Fluent Icons; 130 of them are Fluent's own additions and are
    // absent from Segoe MDL2 Assets, so a machine that falls back to the
    // older font through SymbolThemeFontFamily draws those as blanks.
    "name": "FluentSymbol",
